the corona virus outbreak continues to  advance around the world the number of  cases in China and South Korea keeps  rising but at a slowing rate however the  outbreak in Iran Italy Switzerland and  now France and Germany is growing in  Italy alone we've seen 1492 more cases  overnight and a hundred and two more  deaths here in the UK as of this morning  there were 319 confirmed cases very  sadly this now includes four confirmed  deaths I entirely understand why people  are worried and concerned and we send  our condolences to the families the UK  response is guided by our four point  action plan we continue to work to  contain the virus but we're also taking  action to delay its impact to fund  research and to mitigate its  consequences throughout our approach is  guided by the science that is the  bedrock on which we base all our  decisions our plan sets out what we're  prepared to do and we'll make the right  choices of which action to pursue the  right moment the scientific advice is  clear acting too early creates its own  risks so we will do what is right to  keep people safe guided by the science  will act at the right time and will be  clear and open about our actions and the  reasons for them madam Deputy Speaker  these are the principles that underpin  the very best response to an academic  like this turning to research I can  report to the house that we've made  available a further 46 million to find a  vaccine and develop more rapid  diagnostic tests we'll continue to  support the international effort here at  home the NHS is well prepared with  record numbers of staff record nurses  and record doctors I want to thank all  those involved for their work so far the  number of calls to NHS one-one-one has  increased and we've now added  an extra 700 people to support that  effort one more one online is now  dealing with more inquiries than the  voice calls today public health England  has tested nearly 25,000 people and the  time taken to test is being reduced as  we're bringing in a new system for  faster results  but of course responding to coronavirus  will take a national effort and everyone  must play their part of course that  means government it also means everyone  washing their hands more often and  following public health advice but there  is much more that we can all do both  through volunteering and through support  for those who are the most vulnerable  will shortly bring forward legislative  options to help people and services to  tackle this outbreak the bill will be  temporary and proportionate with  measures that last only as long as  necessary in line with clinical advice  and I can also report that over the  weekend we initiated action to help 120  passengers on the Grand Princess cruise  ship off the coast of California to  assist them in coming home madam Deputy  Speaker we will stop at nothing to get  this response right and I commend this  statement to the house
 

